WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.519
Handoff protocols are what turn message delivery into a completed verified transfer.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.920
Here's what we're covering. Handoff verification, how failures in subagents need to surface back

3
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.000
to the orchestrator, what to preserve when a handoff fails mid-transfer, and how audit logging

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.040
makes interagent communication inspectable. Let's jump in. A sending agent transmits a message and

5
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:31.680
moves on. Did the handoff actually complete? That question is what handoff verification exists to

6
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:38.000
answer. Verification is the step where the sender confirms the receiver has what it needs and is

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.400
ready to proceed before the sender lets go of control. Error propagation is the mechanism that

8
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.080
carries failures inside a subagent back up to the orchestrator, which needs to see them to manage

9
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.799
the workflow at all, and silent failure is the pattern that defeats both. A handoff that looks

10
00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:04.080
successful but quietly loses or corrupts information, raising no error, letting the system proceed on

11
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:10.480
state that's already wrong. Silent failures surface late and trace back hard, which is exactly what

12
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.040
makes them expensive. Verification is what separates a completed handoff from an assumed one.

13
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:22.959
The basic sequence works like this. The sender transmits the handoff message, the receiver

14
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:29.040
acknowledges receipt, the receiver confirms it has the context it needs to proceed, and only then

15
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.480
does the sender relinquish control. Without verification, the sender sends the message and

16
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.080
assumes everything is fine. If the message was malformed, if the receiver never got it, or if the

17
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.400
receiver is missing a piece of context it needs, the sender has no way to know. Verification isn't

18
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.720
a formality. It's the mechanism that makes the handoff's success observable rather than assumed,

19
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.519
and that observability is what gives the orchestrator something to act on when things go

20
00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:04.400
wrong. Failures inside subagents have to reach the orchestrator to be handled. The practical design

21
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.320
requirement is that subagents return structured error payloads rather than null values or empty

22
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.800
responses. A structured error payload carries the failure type, the context the subagent was working

23
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.960
in, and hints about what recovery paths are available. Orchestrators need explicit error

24
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.199
handling paths designed alongside their happy paths, not added as an afterthought. An orchestrator

25
00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:34.880
that receives only success responses from subagents because the subagents are swallowing their own

26
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:41.520
errors internally is operating on an incomplete picture of the workflow. It can't retry, reroute,

27
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.199
escalate to a human, or halt because it never sees the signals it would need to make those decisions.

28
00:02:48.479 --> 00:02:54.160
The contrast between transparent and silent failure is one of the most important distinctions

29
00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.160
in multi-agent system design. When failure is transparent, the subagent returns a structured

30
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.000
error with enough information for the orchestrator to decide how to respond. The system remains

31
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.679
manageable even when things go wrong because the failure is visible. When failure is silent, the

32
00:03:11.679 --> 00:03:18.080
handoff produces a response that looks successful but loses or corrupts something critical. No error

33
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:24.559
is raised, the orchestrator proceeds on incorrect state, and the failure only surfaces downstream,

34
00:03:24.639 --> 00:03:30.320
often far removed from where the actual problem occurred. Diagnosis becomes much harder because

35
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:36.000
there's no error signal to trace. Silent failures are disproportionately damaging,

36
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:43.440
exactly because they masquerade as success. Two error propagation anti-patterns show up frequently

37
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.360
in multi-agent systems. Swallowing errors happens when a subagent catches exceptions internally

38
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.119
and returns empty or partial output rather than an error. The orchestrator receives a response and

39
00:03:55.119 --> 00:04:01.440
assumes success. It never learns the subagent failed. Undifferentiated errors happen when all

40
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.119
failure types return the same generic code. The orchestrator sees an error but can't tell whether

41
00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:13.199
it's a retriable network timeout, a resource limit, or a hard constraint violation that the system

42
00:04:13.199 --> 00:04:19.119
should halt on. Both anti-patterns degrade the orchestrator's ability to manage the workflow.

43
00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:25.600
The fix for both is the same. Subagents need to surface structured typed errors with enough

44
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.839
information for the orchestrator to choose the right response. When a handoff fails mid-transfer,

45
00:04:31.839 --> 00:04:37.279
recovering without losing work requires that state was preserved before the handoff started.

46
00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:42.399
The pattern is to save task state to a durable checkpoint before initiating any handoff.

47
00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:48.320
The checkpoint should reflect the last confirmed good state, not just the most recent attempt.

48
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.359
Retry logic should resume from the checkpoint rather than from the beginning of the workflow.

49
00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:59.200
Without checkpointing, a failed handoff forces the entire workflow to restart from scratch,

50
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:05.119
which is expensive and may not even be safe if any prior steps had side effects. Checkpointing

51
00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:11.040
is especially important in pipeline topologies where a failed handoff in the middle blocks

52
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.000
everything downstream. Preserved state is what gives the system a recovery path that doesn't

53
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.559
require a full restart. Handoff protocols that assume a specific receiver implementation

54
00:05:22.559 --> 00:05:28.640
are fragile for the same reason that tightly coupled schemas are fragile. The protocol

55
00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.959
should specify what behavior the receiver has to exhibit, what inputs it accepts, how it acknowledges

56
00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:42.160
receipt, what it returns on success, and how it surfaces errors. It shouldn't encode assumptions

57
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:48.079
about which specific agent or model will do the work. Any receiver that conforms to the protocol

58
00:05:48.079 --> 00:05:53.920
should be able to handle the handoff. That's the substitutability property, and it's what allows

59
00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:00.720
you to upgrade, replace, or scale out receiver implementations without changing the protocol or

60
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:07.519
rewriting the senders. Designing for substitutability up front is much cheaper than refactoring a tightly

61
00:06:07.519 --> 00:06:14.079
coupled system after it's in production. Audit logging is the primary tool for turning silent

62
00:06:14.079 --> 00:06:20.320
handoff failures into diagnosable ones. A complete handoff log captures the sender identity, the

63
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:26.480
receiver identity, the timestamp, a summary of the payload, the verification result, and any errors.

64
00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:33.040
When a failure surfaces downstream and you need to trace it back, the audit log lets you reconstruct

65
00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.880
exactly what each agent had when it started, what it was asked to produce, and whether its handoffs

66
00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:46.320
were verified. Without that log, silent failures require exhaustive debugging to localize. With it,

67
00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.359
the chain of handoffs is inspectable, and the failure point is usually identifiable without

68
00:06:51.359 --> 00:06:57.200
rerunning the full workflow. Log design is often underweighted in system design discussions,

69
00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:04.399
but in multi-agent systems, it's foundational. The worst multi-agent bugs aren't the ones that

70
00:07:04.399 --> 00:07:10.799
throw errors. It's the transfer that reports success and quietly drops half the context,

71
00:07:10.799 --> 00:07:17.200
so the damage surfaces way downstream with nothing to trace it back to. Confirming the receiver is

72
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.119
really ready before you let go of control turns that silent loss into a visible one,

73
00:07:23.119 --> 00:07:29.440
and visible is the only kind you can fix. That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

